---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import { DAEMON_URL, INSTALL_COMMAND, REPO_URL } from '../site';

// The line the board's "Set up with Claude Code" button sends (board/model.ts).
const SETUP_PROMPT =
  'Run `tabsh setup` and follow the guide it prints, so your hooks keep your card on the tabsh board current.';
const CARGO_INSTALL = `cargo install --git ${REPO_URL}`;
---

<Site
  title="Docs · tabsh"
  description="Install tabsh, connect a coding agent, and see the hooks it adds so its card stays current."
>
  <h1><span class="mark">#</span> DOCS</h1>
  <p>
    A card moves on its own once the agent in that terminal reports what it is doing.
    You do not edit the agent's settings. The agent does, and it tells you which file it changed.
  </p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
    <p><span class="mark">&gt;</span> CONTENTS</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#start">Getting started</a></li>
      <li><a href="#setup">Connect an agent</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hooks">Hooks the agent runs</a></li>
      <li><a href="#done">What you should see</a></li>
      <li><a href="#undo">Undo</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="start"><span class="mark">##</span> GETTING STARTED</h2>
  <p>
    Install tabsh and start it. It opens the app in your browser, already paired
    with the daemon on your machine.
  </p>
  <pre class="cmd"><span class="mark">$</span> {INSTALL_COMMAND}  <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy={INSTALL_COMMAND} aria-label="Copy install command">[copy]</button>
<span class="mark">$</span> tabsh
<span class="mark"># no Homebrew? {CARGO_INSTALL}</span></pre>
  <p>
    That process runs until you close its terminal. To keep the daemon running from
    login, stop it and run <code>brew services start tabsh</code>, then open <code>{DAEMON_URL}</code> once in this browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    Safari, and every iOS browser, cannot reach the daemon from this site. The daemon
    serves the app at <code>http://tabsh.localhost:7681</code>, and this site sends Safari there.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><span class="mark">&gt;</span> Next, <a href="#setup">connect an agent</a> so its card moves on its own.</p>

  <h2 id="setup"><span class="mark">##</span> CONNECT AN AGENT</h2>
  <p>
    Open the board (⌘B). The first time, choose <b>Set up with Claude Code</b>.
    That opens a card and hands the agent the setup guide.
  </p>
  <p>
    Already past that screen, or you use another agent? In a tabsh terminal, paste this to
    Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI or OpenCode:
  </p>
  <pre class="cmd"><span class="mark">&gt;</span> {SETUP_PROMPT}  <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy={SETUP_PROMPT} aria-label="Copy setup prompt">[copy]</button></pre>
  <p>
    Run it inside a tabsh terminal. The hooks look for that terminal's card, and outside
    tabsh they do nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hooks"><span class="mark">##</span> HOOKS THE AGENT RUNS</h2>
  <p>
    <code>tabsh setup</code> prints the guide, and the agent follows it. It backs up the
    config file first, then adds the commands below. It keeps every hook you already have.
    Run the setup again and it updates those tabsh lines instead of adding a second copy.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>When</th><th>Command</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td data-label="When">You send a prompt</td>
        <td data-label="Command"><code>tabsh status in_progress --hook</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td data-label="When">The agent needs your permission or a reply</td>
        <td data-label="Command"><code>tabsh status needs_input --hook --if-not completed</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td data-label="When">The agent's turn ends</td>
        <td data-label="Command"><code>tabsh status needs_input --hook --if-not completed --note 'Agent finished its turn'</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td data-label="When">The task is done</td>
        <td data-label="Command"><code>tabsh status completed --note '&lt;one line: what it did&gt;'</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    The last row is not a hook. The agent adds one rule to the instructions it reads
    every session, and runs that command itself when it decides the task is done.
    It never sets another status, and it never archives a card. Archiving stays yours.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--hook</code> prints nothing and always exits 0, including outside a tabsh
    terminal, so the same lines are safe in every shell. It reads the event's JSON.
    A <code>message</code> field becomes the card's note. A <code>session_id</code>
    is how tabsh resumes that conversation after a restart. <code>--if-not completed</code> leaves a finished card where it is when a later hook fires.
  </p>
  <p>Each agent writes those commands into its own file:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Agent</th><th>File</th><th>Events</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td data-label="Agent">Claude Code</td>
        <td data-label="File"><code>~/.claude/settings.json</code></td>
        <td data-label="Events">UserPromptSubmit, Notification, Stop</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td data-label="Agent">Gemini CLI</td>
        <td data-label="File"><code>~/.gemini/settings.json</code></td>
        <td data-label="Events">BeforeAgent, Notification, AfterAgent</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td data-label="Agent">Codex</td>
        <td data-label="File"><code>~/.codex/config.toml</code></td>
        <td data-label="Events"><code>notify</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td data-label="Agent">OpenCode</td>
        <td data-label="File"><code>~/.config/opencode/plugins/tabsh.ts</code></td>
        <td data-label="Events">session started, permission asked, execution ended</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    For Claude Code and Gemini CLI those events follow the table above, in order:
    your prompt, the agent needing you, then the turn ending. Codex has a single <code>notify</code> hook and no event for the moment you send a prompt, so the
    agent leaves that command out. OpenCode's file is a plugin. Its server never
    sees the terminal, so the plugin finds the card by the conversation tabsh named.
    Permission asked uses the note <code>OpenCode needs your permission</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent checks its own current docs for those event names, and the commands
    use the <code>tabsh</code> binary it found. Its report names the path it wrote.
    If that path is a build folder under <code>target/</code>, the report says so,
    because the hooks would point at it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A desktop notification and a chime fire when a hook moves a card to Needs input
    or Completed and that terminal is not the one on screen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="done"><span class="mark">##</span> WHAT YOU SHOULD SEE</h2>
  <p>The agent reports back:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><b>The file it changed</b>, and a <code>.bak</code> copy beside it.</li>
    <li><b>Which of those commands it connected</b>, and which its agent has no way to report.</li>
    <li><b>How to undo it.</b></li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    While it checks the connection, the card moves to Needs input with the note <code>setup test</code>, then back to In progress. If the card never moves, paste
    that report back into the same terminal and ask the agent to fix the connection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="undo"><span class="mark">##</span> UNDO</h2>
  <p>
    Ask the agent to remove the tabsh hooks. Its report names the file.
    The entries to remove are the ones whose command contains <code> status </code>, <code>--hook</code> and the word <code>tabsh</code>, plus the instruction that
    marks a card completed. OpenCode's wiring is the one plugin: delete <code>~/.config/opencode/plugins/tabsh.ts</code>.
  </p>

  <p class="sig end">~*~ EOF ~*~</p>
</Site>

<Fragment slot="after">
  <script>
    for (const btn of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]')) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(btn.dataset.copy ?? '');
        btn.textContent = '[copied]';
        setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = '[copy]'), 1500);
      });
    }
  </script>
</Fragment>

<style>
  .cmd { padding: .75rem 1rem; border-radius: 4px; background: var(--code-bg); white-space: pre-wrap; }
  .copy {
    padding: 0; border: 0; background: none; color: var(--link); font: inherit; letter-spacing: inherit; cursor: pointer;
  }
  .copy:hover { color: var(--fg); }
  .toc ol, .steps { padding-left: 2.25rem; }
  .toc li::marker, .steps li::marker { color: var(--muted); }
  .toc li + li, .steps li + li { margin-top: .5rem; }
  .toc { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .scroll { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0; }
  th, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: .5rem 1rem .5rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); font-weight: 400; }
  th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; }
  td code { white-space: normal; }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .scroll { overflow-x: visible; }
    table, tbody, tr, td { display: block; min-width: 0; width: auto; }
    thead { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0); white-space: nowrap; }
    tr { padding: .75rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
    td { border: 0; padding: 0; }
    td + td { margin-top: .35rem; }
    td::before { content: attr(data-label); display: block; color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; }
    td code { white-space: normal; }
  }
</style>
